solution:
Transport: input and output happen simultaneously, so injection capacity equals withdrawal capacity and one number - 'the capacity' - describes the facility. Storage: input and output are separated in time, so three parameters are needed and they can be chosen independently within physical limits - injection capacity, withdrawal capacity, and volume/quantity, i.e. the working volume. A cavern that fills in 60 days but empties in 20 is entirely normal; that asymmetry cannot even be expressed with a transport capacity.

Units for transport:
natural gas - volume/time (m3/h), or converted with the calorific value into energy/time = capacity (kWh/h);
electricity - energy/time = capacity (MW);
oil and products by pipeline - volume/time;
oil and products by other modes - volume (limited), i.e. per cargo or per tank;
coal - mass (limited), i.e. per shipload or per train.

Units for storage:
natural gas - volume, and volume/time (m3 of working gas, m3/h for injection and withdrawal);
electricity - capacity in the sense of current times time, or energy (MWh);
oil and products - volume, and volume/time;
coal - mass, and mass/time.

Two exam traps live here. First, gas is booked in volume but paid in energy: multiply by the gross calorific value before you price anything. Second, a rate (per hour, per day) is not a quantity - the storage bill contains both, a capacity price on the two rates and a volume price on the working gas.
